{% doc %}
  A group: a flex container that holds other blocks and carries its own size,
  background, border and link.

  ## Alignment always means the same thing

  The hardest part of a layout control is that "Left" has to mean left whether
  the group stacks or sits side by side — but in CSS it is `align-items` in one
  case and `justify-content` in the other. Exposing the CSS properties directly
  is why so many builders have an Alignment control that moves things vertically
  half the time.

  So the merchant gets two controls with fixed meanings:

    Alignment  always the horizontal axis
    Position   always the vertical axis

  and this snippet writes them to two custom properties, `--group-inline` and
  `--group-block`. `base.css` then decides which CSS property reads which, based
  on direction. One place knows about the swap; the merchant never does.

  ## Distribution is separate from alignment

  "Space between" is not an alignment — it is what happens to the *gaps*, and it
  only exists on the main axis. Rather than adding a fourth option to Alignment
  that silently does nothing when the group is stacked, it is its own control,
  shown only when the group is horizontal. No option in this block is ever
  inert.

  ## Width, and why Fill is not 100%

  Inside a horizontal parent, "Fill" has to mean *take the space that is left*,
  which is `flex: 1`, not `width: 100%` — the latter overflows as soon as there
  is a sibling. Fit is `flex: 0 0 auto`, Custom is `flex: 0 0 <n>%`. All three
  behave correctly in a stacked parent too, where there is no space to compete
  for.

  Mobile takes its own width because the desktop answer is usually wrong on a
  phone: two 50% columns want to be one 100% column, and that is a different
  value rather than a scale of the same one.

  ## The link

  A group that is a link cannot be an `<a>`: it contains blocks, and blocks
  contain buttons and links of their own — an `<a>` inside an `<a>` is invalid
  and browsers recover from it unpredictably.

  Instead the group stays a `<div>` and an absolutely positioned `<a>` covers
  it. Anything genuinely interactive inside raises itself above that overlay in
  `base.css`, so a button inside a linked group is still a button and the rest
  of the surface is still the link.

  ## Built on, rather than forked

  Three parameters exist only so another block can be a group without copying
  one: `extra_classes` to name itself, `tag` to be a different element, and
  `attributes` for whatever that element needs. `blocks/image-card.liquid` uses
  all three — it is a group whose background is the picture and whose wrapper is
  a `<motion-effect>`, and it adds no layout code of its own.

  The alternative is a second flex container with its own alignment swap, its
  own width pair and its own idea of what Fill means. There is one of those in
  the theme and this is it.

  @param {object} settings - Block settings
  @param {string} [children] - Rendered child blocks
  @param {string} [extra_classes] - Extra classes for the wrapper, so a block
    built on top of a group can name itself without forking this snippet
  @param {string} [tag] - Wrapper element. Default: `div`
  @param {string} [attributes] - Raw attributes for the wrapper
  @param {string} [styles] - Declarations appended to the wrapper's own `style`,
    because a second `style` attribute on the same tag is dropped by the browser
  @param {boolean} [priority] - Eager-load the background image. Above the fold only
  @param {string} [placeholder] - Placeholder to draw when the background image is
    not uploaded yet. Off unless a caller asks for it
  @param {string} [shopify_attributes] - Editor attributes

  @example
    {% render 'group', children: children, settings: block.settings,
       shopify_attributes: block.shopify_attributes %}
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign direction = settings.direction | default: 'column'
  assign is_row = false
  if direction == 'row'
    assign is_row = true
  endif

  # ---- axes -------------------------------------------------------------
  # Written as flexbox keywords here so the stylesheet does not need a lookup
  # table of its own. `start` and `end` rather than `flex-start`: the modern
  # keywords work for both `align-items` and `justify-content`, which is what
  # lets one property serve both axes.
  case settings.alignment
    when 'center'
      assign inline_axis = 'center'
    when 'end'
      assign inline_axis = 'end'
    else
      assign inline_axis = 'start'
  endcase

  case settings.position
    when 'center'
      assign block_axis = 'center'
    when 'end'
      assign block_axis = 'end'
    else
      assign block_axis = 'start'
  endcase

  # Distribution replaces the inline alignment, because both land on
  # `justify-content` and the later one would win arbitrarily. Only meaningful
  # on a row, so ignored entirely on a column.
  #
  # Coerced with `default` and compared against a real string rather than tested
  # with `!= blank`. An unset setting is nil in Shopify and Undefined in other
  # Liquid engines, and only one of those is blank — a difference that silently
  # emitted `--group-inline:` with no value.

  # The alignment on its own, captured before a distribution can replace it.
  # See `--group-align` below for what reads it.
  assign alignment_axis = inline_axis

  assign distribution = settings.distribution | default: 'none'
  if is_row and distribution != 'none'
    assign inline_axis = distribution
  endif

  # ---- size -------------------------------------------------------------
  assign width_pct = settings.width_custom | default: 100 | plus: 0
  assign width_mobile_pct = settings.width_custom_mobile | default: 100 | plus: 0

  # Two properties, not one.
  #
  # `flex` only means anything to a flex *item*, and a group is only a flex item
  # when its parent is another group. At the top level of a section — inside
  # `.rich-text__inner`, a `.container`, anywhere that is not `display: flex` —
  # a `flex` declaration is inert, so Fit and Custom did nothing and every group
  # rendered full width. That is the bug.
  #
  # So each option also writes an `inline-size`. Inside a flex row the
  # `flex-basis` wins for the initial main size and the width is ignored; outside
  # one the width is all there is. Both agree, so neither context is a special
  # case.
  case settings.width
    when 'fill'
      assign width_value = '1 1 0%'
      assign width_size = '100%'
    when 'custom'
      assign width_value = '0 0 ' | append: width_pct | append: '%'
      assign width_size = width_pct | append: '%'
    else
      assign width_value = '0 0 auto'
      assign width_size = 'fit-content'
  endcase

  case settings.width_mobile
    when 'fill'
      assign width_mobile_value = '1 1 0%'
      assign width_mobile_size = '100%'
    when 'custom'
      assign width_mobile_value = '0 0 ' | append: width_mobile_pct | append: '%'
      assign width_mobile_size = width_mobile_pct | append: '%'
    else
      assign width_mobile_value = '0 0 auto'
      assign width_mobile_size = 'fit-content'
  endcase

  case settings.height
    when 'fill'
      assign height_value = '100%'
    when 'custom'
      assign custom_height = settings.height_custom | default: 300 | plus: 0
      assign height_value = custom_height | append: 'px'
    else
      assign height_value = 'auto'
  endcase

  # ---- border -----------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Opacity goes into the colour, not onto the element: `opacity` on the group
  # would fade its contents along with the frame.
  #
  # With no colour picked the border is `currentColor`, which the colour filters
  # cannot take an alpha channel from — there is no channel to read until the
  # scheme resolves it in the browser. `color-mix` does the same fade at that
  # point instead, so both paths honour the slider.
  # Any style the caller offers, not just solid. The Group block lists None and
  # Solid; `blocks/card-content.liquid` adds Dashed, and the difference between
  # them is one keyword in the declaration rather than a second border system.
  assign border_line = settings.border_style | default: 'none'
  assign border_width = 0
  if border_line != 'none'
    assign border_width = settings.border_width | default: 1 | plus: 0

    assign border_percent = settings.border_opacity | default: 100 | plus: 0
    if settings.border_color == blank
      assign border_paint = 'color-mix(in srgb, currentColor ' | append: border_percent | append: '%, transparent)'
    else
      assign border_alpha = border_percent | divided_by: 100.0
      assign border_paint = settings.border_color | color_modify: 'alpha', border_alpha
    endif
  endif

  assign radius = settings.border_radius | default: 0 | plus: 0

  # ---- gaps -------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # `gap` is the original single control and is what every group saved before
  # this change is configured with. The two axes layer on top of it: each falls
  # back to `gap` when the merchant has not moved it, so an existing group
  # renders exactly as it did and a new one gets the axis it was given. `plus: 0`
  # coerces the range to a number — `default` alone does not replace a
  # deliberate 0, which is a value a merchant can legitimately want here.
  assign gap_base = settings.gap | default: 16 | plus: 0
  assign gap_x = settings.gap_horizontal | default: gap_base | plus: 0
  assign gap_y = settings.gap_vertical | default: gap_base | plus: 0
  assign gap_x_mobile = settings.gap_horizontal_mobile | default: gap_x | plus: 0
  assign gap_y_mobile = settings.gap_vertical_mobile | default: gap_y | plus: 0

  # ---- mobile layout ----------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Swipe is CSS scroll-snap, not a carousel component. The children are already
  # laid out in a flex row; making that row scroll and snap is four declarations
  # and works with no script, no resize observer and no second set of markup for
  # small screens. A `<swiper-carousel>` here would mean cloning the children
  # into slides, which is a different DOM on a phone than on a desktop — and the
  # blocks inside a group are authored by the merchant, not by the theme.
  assign swipe_on_mobile = settings.swipe_on_mobile | default: false
  assign custom_column_width_mobile = settings.custom_column_width_mobile | default: false
  assign column_width_mobile = settings.column_width_mobile | default: 76 | plus: 0
  assign column_order = settings.column_order | default: 0 | plus: 0

  assign sticky_desktop = settings.sticky_desktop | default: false

  # ---- flags ------------------------------------------------------------
  assign background_media = settings.background_media | default: 'none'
  assign has_media = false
  if background_media != 'none'
    assign has_media = true
  endif

  assign link_url = settings.link | default: ''
  assign has_link = false
  if link_url != ''
    assign has_link = true
  endif

  # ---- classes ----------------------------------------------------------
  assign classes = 'block-group'
  assign classes = classes | append: ' block-group--' | append: direction
  if settings.stack_on_mobile and is_row
    assign classes = classes | append: ' block-group--stack-mobile'
  endif
  if has_media
    assign classes = classes | append: ' has-background-media'
  endif
  if has_link
    assign classes = classes | append: ' block-group--linked'
  endif
  if swipe_on_mobile
    assign classes = classes | append: ' block-group--swipe-mobile'
  endif
  if sticky_desktop
    assign classes = classes | append: ' block-group--sticky'
  endif

  # Appended last so a caller's own modifier always sits after the group's own
  # classes, and a block built on this snippet can style itself without forking
  # it.
  if extra_classes != blank
    assign classes = classes | append: ' ' | append: extra_classes
  endif

  # ---- motion -----------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # A group animates itself when it carries the setting, and staggers its own
  # children when the merchant asks for one. It lives here rather than in each
  # block because every block built on this snippet is a container, and a
  # container that animates is the same decision each time.
  #
  # `children: true` is what makes **Stagger between items** mean anything: the
  # blocks inside are the items, and staggering the container against itself
  # would be one element with a delay on it.
  #
  # A caller that brings its own — `blocks/image-card.liquid` animates as an
  # image, not as text — passes them through `attributes` and has no
  # `animation_text` setting, so nothing is emitted twice.
  capture motion_attrs
    if settings.animation_text
      render 'motion-attrs', kind: 'text', block: settings, children: true
    endif
  endcapture
  assign motion_attrs = motion_attrs | strip

  assign inherit_color_scheme = settings.inherit_color_scheme | default: false
  assign scheme = settings.color_scheme | default: ''
  if inherit_color_scheme == false and scheme != ''
    assign classes = classes | append: ' color-scheme-container color-' | append: scheme
  endif
-%}

{%- capture group_style -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Declarations, not an attribute. `{% render 'spacing' %}` emits its own
    `style="…"`, and this element already has one — a tag with two `style`
    attributes keeps the first and the browser drops the rest, so every padding
    slider on the group block was moving a value the page never saw. The
    `data-block-spacing` hook goes on by hand below, since that is the selector
    `base.css` applies the padding through.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- render 'spacing', block: settings, declarations: true -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    `--group-gap` is kept as the single-value shorthand some other markup still
    reads. The two axes are what `base.css` uses for this element, in the CSS
    `gap: <row> <column>` order.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  --group-gap: {{ gap_base }}px;
  --group-gap-x: {{ gap_x }}px;
  --group-gap-y: {{ gap_y }}px;
  --group-gap-x-mobile: {{ gap_x_mobile }}px;
  --group-gap-y-mobile: {{ gap_y_mobile }}px;
  --group-order: {{ column_order }};
  {%- if swipe_on_mobile and custom_column_width_mobile -%}
    --group-swipe-width: {{ column_width_mobile }}%;
  {%- endif -%}
  --group-inline: {{ inline_axis }};
  {%- comment -%}
    The alignment on its own, with no distribution folded into it.

    `--group-inline` is the main axis and legitimately carries `space-between`
    on a row, where it lands on `justify-content`. Once that row stacks on a
    phone the same value lands on `align-items`, and `align-items:
    space-between` is not valid — the declaration is dropped and the stack
    falls back to `stretch`. That is how a right-hand button ends up spanning
    the full width on a phone.

    So the raw alignment travels alongside it and the mobile rule prefers this
    one. A group saved before this property existed has no `--group-align` and
    falls back to `--group-inline`, exactly as it behaved before.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  --group-align: {{ alignment_axis }};
  --group-block: {{ block_axis }};
  --group-width: {{ width_value }};
  --group-width-size: {{ width_size }};
  --group-width-mobile: {{ width_mobile_value }};
  --group-width-mobile-size: {{ width_mobile_size }};
  --group-height: {{ height_value }};
  --group-radius: {{ radius }}px;
  {%- if border_width > 0 -%}
    --group-border-width: {{ border_width }}px;
    --group-border-style: {{ border_line }};
    --group-border-color: {{ border_paint }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    A frosted panel. The blur is the group's because a panel that blurs what is
    behind it is a container decision, not a new kind of container — the card in
    the FAQ banner is a group on a photograph, and so is every other one.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign backdrop_blur = settings.backdrop_blur | default: 0 | plus: 0 -%}
  {%- if backdrop_blur > 0 -%}
    --group-backdrop-blur: {{ backdrop_blur }}px;
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- assign background_color = settings.background_color | default: '' -%}
  {%- if background_color != '' and has_media == false -%}
    --group-background: {{ background_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Last, so a caller building on this snippet can override a property it set
    rather than having to fork the whole capture to change one value.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {{- styles -}}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- liquid
  assign group_tag = tag
  if group_tag == blank
    assign group_tag = 'div'
    if motion_attrs != blank
      assign group_tag = 'motion-effect'
    endif
  endif
-%}
<{{ group_tag }}
  class="{{ classes }}"
  data-block-spacing
  style="{{ group_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
  {{ motion_attrs }}
  {{ attributes }}
  {{ shopify_attributes }}
>
  {%- if has_media -%}
    {% render 'background-media', settings: settings, priority: priority, placeholder: placeholder %}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    The children sit in their own layer so they stay above the background media
    and above the link overlay. Without it a group with a background would put
    its own backdrop over its contents.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="block-group__content">
    {{- children -}}
  </div>

  {%- if has_link -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      `aria-label` falls back to the URL only if the merchant left it blank,
      which is worse than a real label but far better than a link a screen
      reader announces as nothing at all.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <a
      class="block-group__link"
      href="{{ link_url }}"
      {% if settings.link_new_tab %}target="_blank" rel="noopener"{% endif %}
      aria-label="{{ settings.link_label | default: link_url | escape }}"
    ></a>
  {%- endif -%}
</{{ group_tag }}>